          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T9 or T6</h3>
          <p>
            Incline, speed and the frame and motor warranty are identical, so the extra money buys three things:
            two more inches of width, 25 lb more rated capacity and a higher motor rating on the same test basis.
            The colour console is a nicety. Pay for the T9 if you are a runner over about six feet, broad, or
            within reach of the T6's 325 lb limit. Keep the money if you mainly walk or incline-walk, or if
            floor space is tight, since the T6 folds shorter and flat.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T9 or T16</h3>
          <p>
            The T16's case, on Bowflex's listing, is the 16-inch touchscreen, which Bowflex says can browse the
            web and run streaming apps. If a big built-in screen is what will keep you training, it is the
            Bowflex to look at; we have not verified its other figures, so check them before you buy. If you
            are happy with a phone or tablet on the media holder, the T9 gives you the same deck width, incline
            and frame and motor cover for less.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand overview</a> covers the whole range, including the Max Trainer machines and the 2024 change of ownership to
            Johnson Health Tech.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Alternatives Outside Bowflex</h2>
<p>
            The T9's figures put it against several well-known machines, and the right comparison depends on
            what you value.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a big screen and classes, not an open console:</strong> the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</a> has a 22 by 60-inch deck, decline to -3%, a 16-inch pivoting touchscreen and lifetime motor cover,
            at a higher price and built around a paid iFIT membership. Our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart treadmills</a> roundup sets out the trade-off between a screen-led machine and an open one like the T9.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you will never pay for an app:</strong> the T9 already suits you; our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a> covers others that work fully on their own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need decline:</strong> the T9 has none, so look at the Commercial 1750 or the
            machines in our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mainly walk:</strong> a walking-focused machine does the job for less; our <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill roundup</a> covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the Bowflex T9</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you run and want room on the belt.</strong> The 22-inch deck is the T9's defining
            feature, and for tall or broad runners it is the right reason to choose it over the T6 or a typical
            20-inch folding treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavier user.</strong> A 350 lb rating with a lifetime frame and motor
            warranty gives more margin than the T6's 325 lb, and the motor cover is reassurance for long, steep
            sessions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want incline without a subscription.</strong> Fifteen percent of motorised
            lift and a console that works on its own, with the option to connect JRNY, Peloton or Zwift, is an
            unusual combination at this price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Think twice if heart-rate training through an app is central to you</strong>, because of the
            Bluetooth limitation both reviews report, and if you want a soft deck, because both describe it as
            firm.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose the T6 if you mainly walk</strong> or need it to fold smaller, and the T16 if a
            built-in touchscreen is what will keep you on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it on sale.</strong> It was $1,399 against $1,799 regular when we checked, and Bowflex
            discounts often. Compare the delivered price, including the delivery option you need, before you
            order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Bowflex T9</h2>
          <p>The T9 is a straightforward machine with a few figures that are easy to misread. These are the errors that cost buyers money, space or patience.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing 3.5 HP with another brand’s CHP</h3>
              <p>Bowflex says its 3.5 HP is a test-setting rating that does not reflect operational horsepower. Setting it against a competitor’s 3.5 CHP and calling them equal, or calling either stronger, compares two different measurements. Use the lifetime motor warranty as the confidence signal instead.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Planning app-based heart-rate training without checking the Bluetooth</h3>
              <p>Both independent reviews we read report that the T9 cannot connect a heart-rate strap and an app at the same time. If that is how you train, pair the strap to the app instead, and test your setup early in the 30-day return window.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the ceiling</h3>
              <p>Bowflex’s guidance is the user’s height plus 20.75 inches, which puts a 6 ft 5 in runner above a standard 8-foot ceiling. Tall runners are the T9’s best buyers and the ones this affects most, so measure the lowest point above the belt before ordering.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying the regular price</h3>
              <p>The T9 was $1,799 regular and $1,399 on sale on the day we checked, and Bowflex discounts often. Watch the price, and compare the delivered total, including the delivery option you need, before you buy.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Buy the Bowflex T9 at or near its sale price if you run, are tall or
            broad, or want more than the T6's 325 lb rating.</strong> A 22 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline, a
            350 lb rating, a console that needs no subscription and lifetime frame and motor cover are a strong
            set of published figures, and independent reviews back them up. Read the 3.5 HP as a test rating
            and plan for the one-year labour term.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walkers should save the difference with the <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Bowflex T6</a>,
            and anyone who trains by heart rate through an app should check the Bluetooth workaround before the
            return window closes.
          </p>
        </div>

